QUESTIONS = [
    # CH48 Election Commission – L1 (30)
    {"text":"The Election Commission of India is established under which Article of the Constitution?","options":["Article 320","Article 323","Article 324","Article 326"],"correct_answer":"Article 324","explanation":"Article 324 vests the superintendence, direction and control of preparation of electoral rolls and the conduct of elections to Parliament, State Legislatures, and the offices of President and Vice-President in the Election Commission of India.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Chief Election Commissioner (CEC) can be removed from office in the same manner as:","options":["A Governor","A Supreme Court judge","A High Court judge","The Attorney General"],"correct_answer":"A Supreme Court judge","explanation":"Article 324(5) provides that the CEC shall not be removed from office except in the manner and on the grounds like a Supreme Court judge — by Presidential order following addresses by both Houses of Parliament on grounds of proved misbehaviour or incapacity.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Election Commission of India supervises elections to which of the following?","options":["Parliament and State Legislatures only","Parliament, State Legislatures, President, and Vice-President","All elections including Panchayats and Municipalities","Parliament and State Legislatures including local bodies in UTs"],"correct_answer":"Parliament, State Legislatures, President, and Vice-President","explanation":"Article 324 covers elections to Parliament, State Legislatures, and the offices of the President and Vice-President. Panchayat and Municipal elections are conducted by State Election Commissions (Article 243K).","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Election Commissioner (other than the CEC) can be removed:","options":["In the same manner as the CEC — only by Parliamentary address","On the recommendation of the CEC alone","On the recommendation of the CEC — by the President","By the President without any recommendation"],"correct_answer":"On the recommendation of the CEC alone","explanation":"Article 324(5) provides that an Election Commissioner shall not be removed except on the recommendation of the CEC. This makes the CEC the protector of ECs' independence. However, the CEC himself/herself cannot be removed except through Parliamentary address.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Model Code of Conduct (MCC) enforced by the ECI is:","options":["A statutory code under the Representation of the People Act","A constitutional provision under Article 324","A voluntary code with no legal backing but enforced through ECI's Article 324 powers","An executive order made by the Union Cabinet"],"correct_answer":"A voluntary code with no legal backing but enforced through ECI's Article 324 powers","explanation":"The MCC has no statutory basis — it is a set of guidelines evolved through consensus from 1968 onwards. The ECI enforces it using its plenary powers under Article 324, making it effective despite having no direct legal backing.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The ECI was a single-member body (only CEC) from 1950 until it was made multi-member. When was it permanently made a three-member body?","options":["1989","1993","1996","2001"],"correct_answer":"1993","explanation":"In 1989, two additional Election Commissioners were added just before elections (and removed after). In 1993, T.N. Seshan's era saw the permanent expansion to three members (CEC + 2 ECs) through the Election Commission (Conditions of Service of Election Commissioner) Amendment Act.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The ECI has power to recognize political parties and allot election symbols. This power is derived from:","options":["Articles 324 and the Representation of the People Act","The Constitution directly under Article 19(1)(c)","The Companies Act for registered associations","The Election Symbols (Reservation and Allotment) Order under Article 324"],"correct_answer":"The Election Symbols (Reservation and Allotment) Order under Article 324","explanation":"The ECI allots symbols and recognises parties under the Election Symbols (Reservation and Allotment) Order 1968, issued by the ECI under its plenary powers under Article 324. This is a quasi-legislative order.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which test is used by ECI to recognise a political party as a 'National Party'?","options":["At least 5% votes in 4 or more states in Lok Sabha elections","6% votes in 4+ states OR 2% Lok Sabha seats from 3+ states  OR recognition as State Party in 4+ states","At least 10 Lok Sabha seats","Registered in at least 10 states with branches"],"correct_answer":"6% votes in 4+ states OR 2% Lok Sabha seats from 3+ states  OR recognition as State Party in 4+ states","explanation":"ECI's criteria for National Party (any one criterion): (a) 6% valid votes in 4+ states + 4 Lok Sabha seats, (b) 2% of Lok Sabha seats from 3+ states, or (c) recognised as State Party in 4+ states.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The ECI can de-recognise or freeze a political party. This power is exercised using:","options":["Powers under Article 102","Powers under the 10th Schedule","Powers under the Election Symbols Order 1968","Powers under the RPA 1951 Section 123"],"correct_answer":"Powers under the Election Symbols Order 1968","explanation":"De-recognition or freezing of party symbols is done by the ECI using the Election Symbols (Reservation and Allotment) Order 1968 — a quasi-legislative instrument issued under Article 324.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The EVM (Electronic Voting Machine) system was first used in all constituencies in which general election?","options":["1999","2004","2009","2014"],"correct_answer":"2004","explanation":"EVMs were used on a pilot basis in select constituencies from 1982. The 2004 General Election was the first in which EVMs were used in ALL constituencies across India.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"VVPAT (Voter Verifiable Paper Audit Trail) was made mandatory in all polling stations from which election?","options":["2014 General Elections","2017 State Elections","2019 General Elections","2022 State Elections"],"correct_answer":"2019 General Elections","explanation":"VVPAT machines were piloted in 2014 (certain constituencies) and made mandatory across all polling stations for the 2019 General Elections following a Supreme Court order in N. Chandrasekhar Pillai v. ECI (2019).","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Representation of the People Act (RPA) 1950 primarily deals with:","options":["Qualification of candidates for elections","Preparation of electoral rolls and delimitation","Conduct of elections and corrupt practices","Registration of political parties"],"correct_answer":"Preparation of electoral rolls and delimitation","explanation":"The RPA 1950 deals with qualifications for voting, preparation of electoral rolls, allocation of seats in Parliament and State Assemblies, and delimitation. RPA 1951 deals with conduct of elections, corrupt practices, and election disputes.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which authority is responsible for delimitation of constituencies?","options":["Election Commission of India","Delimitation Commission constituted by Central Government","The President of India directly","The Law Commission of India"],"correct_answer":"Delimitation Commission constituted by Central Government","explanation":"Delimitation of constituencies is done by Delimitation Commissions constituted by the Central Government under the Delimitation Commission Act. ECI implements but does not delimit. The Delimitation Commission works alongside ECI.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is the significance of the Supreme Court ruling in T.N. Seshan v. Union of India (1995)?","options":["It upheld the power of ECI to derecognise political parties","It upheld the multi-member composition of ECI and clarified that all three members have co-equal status","It struck down EVMs as unconstitutional","It held MCC to be legally binding"],"correct_answer":"It upheld the multi-member composition of ECI and clarified that all three members have co-equal status","explanation":"T.N. Seshan v. Union of India (1995) settled the constitutional status of the multi-member ECI — the SC held the two ECs have co-equal status with the CEC and ECI functions by majority decision.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The minimum age to vote in elections to the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies is:","options":["21 years","18 years","25 years","16 years"],"correct_answer":"18 years","explanation":"The 61st Constitutional Amendment (1988) reduced the voting age from 21 to 18 years. Article 326 now provides that elections to Lok Sabha and State Assemblies shall be on the basis of adult suffrage — universal suffrage for all citizens above 18.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The time limit within which an election petition must be filed after election results are declared is:","options":["30 days","45 days","60 days","90 days"],"correct_answer":"45 days","explanation":"Under Section 81 of the Representation of the People Act, 1951, an election petition must be presented before the High Court within 45 days from the date of election of the returned candidate or, if multiple dates, from the last date of publication of results.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The ECI's 'NOTA' option on EVMs was introduced after which Supreme Court ruling?","options":["People's Union for Civil Liberties v. Union of India (2013)","Association for Democratic Reforms v. Union of India (2002)","N. Chandrasekharan Pillai v. ECI (2019)","S. Subramaniam Balaji v. Government of Tamil Nadu (2013)"],"correct_answer":"People's Union for Civil Liberties v. Union of India (2013)","explanation":"The SC in PUCL v. Union of India (2013) directed ECI to introduce the NOTA (None of the Above) option in EVMs to enable voters to reject all candidates while maintaining secrecy.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The ECI can postpone elections in a constituency. What is the basis for this power?","options":["Article 324 plenary powers","Representation of the People Act Section 153","Both Article 324 and RPA Section 153","A specific constitutional provision for natural calamities"],"correct_answer":"Both Article 324 and RPA Section 153","explanation":"The ECI can postpone elections under its plenary powers in Article 324 and also under Section 153 of the RPA 1951 which allows adjournment of poll in case of election duty-related events. Both provide the legal basis.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"ECI's power to disqualify a voter for corrupt practices is exercised under:","options":["Article 102 of the Constitution","Section 8A of the Representation of the People Act 1951","Article 326 directly","Section 16 of the RPA 1950"],"correct_answer":"Section 8A of the Representation of the People Act 1951","explanation":"Section 8A of the RPA 1951 deals with disqualification for corrupt practices — the SC decision on a petition is sent to the President/Governor for disqualification. ECI does not directly disqualify voters for corrupt practices.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The concept of 'paid news' in elections — where media coverage is bought but not disclosed as an advertisement — is addressed by ECI through:","options":["A specific law passed by Parliament","ECI guidelines under Article 324 and the MCC framework","Judicial orders by the SC","The Press Council of India guidelines"],"correct_answer":"ECI guidelines under Article 324 and the MCC framework","explanation":"There is no specific Parliament law on paid news. ECI has issued guidelines and set up monitoring systems under its Article 324 powers and as part of the Model Code of Conduct to address paid news as an election malpractice.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The 'recognized national parties' are entitled to which of the following exclusive privileges?","options":["No campaign finance limit","Reserved election symbol throughout the country","Free airtime on both All India Radio and Doordarshan","Both a reserved symbol and free broadcast time"],"correct_answer":"Both a reserved symbol and free broadcast time","explanation":"Recognized national parties get: (1) a reserved symbol nationwide, (2) free broadcast time on AIR and Doordarshan proportional to their past performance, (3) access to electoral rolls, and (4) star campaigner facility.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which body adjudicates election disputes for Parliament and State Legislatures after election results?","options":["Election Commission of India","The Supreme Court of India","The High Court having jurisdiction","A special Election Tribunal under RPA"],"correct_answer":"The High Court having jurisdiction","explanation":"Under Article 329(b) and the RPA 1951, election disputes (election petitions) are tried by the High Court having jurisdiction over the constituency. Appeals from HC decisions go to the Supreme Court.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The 'electoral bond' scheme was introduced in 2018 to fund political parties. The SC struck it down in 2024 on the ground that:","options":["Only ECI can collect donations for parties","It violated the right to information about political funding (Article 19(1)(a))","Parliament had no power to legislate on party funding","It violated free and fair elections under Article 324"],"correct_answer":"It violated the right to information about political funding (Article 19(1)(a))","explanation":"The SC in Association for Democratic Reforms v. Union of India (2024) unanimously struck down the Electoral Bond Scheme as unconstitutional — it held voters have a right to know who funds political parties under Article 19(1)(a).","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which unique constitutional power does ECI have that no other election body possesses?","options":["Power to impose President's Rule during elections","Power to countermand elections in case of booth capturing (Section 58A)","Power to suspend fundamental rights of candidates","Power to recall elected representatives"],"correct_answer":"Power to countermand elections in case of booth capturing (Section 58A)","explanation":"Under Section 58A of the RPA 1951, ECI can countermand polls (cancel and reschedule) in constituencies where booth capturing occurs — a unique power to protect the integrity of elections.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The expenditure limit for Lok Sabha election candidates is determined by:","options":["The Constitution directly","ECI through a statutory rule under RPA","The Central Government by notification under RPA","Parliament directly by amendment to RPA"],"correct_answer":"The Central Government by notification under RPA","explanation":"Election expenditure limits are set by the Central Government by notification (not the Constitution or Parliament directly). The ECI recommends revisions. Currently the limit for LS candidates is Rs 95 lakh in large states.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which statement correctly describes the ECI's power under Article 324 in relation to constitutional gaps?","options":["ECI's Article 324 powers are limited to what is expressly stated in the RPA","ECI's plenary powers under Article 324 fill constitutional gaps where no specific law exists","ECI cannot act unless Parliament enacts a specific law on each election matter","ECI can override Parliamentary laws under Article 324"],"correct_answer":"ECI's plenary powers under Article 324 fill constitutional gaps where no specific law exists","explanation":"The SC (Mohinder Singh Gill 1978, T.N. Seshan 1995) held that Article 324 grants ECI plenary powers to act where Parliamentary law is silent — ECI is not limited only to what statute explicitly permits. It can fill gaps in existing law.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"Under the Conduct of Election Rules 1961, how long before an election are campaigning restrictions ('silence period') imposed?","options":["12 hours","24 hours","48 hours","72 hours"],"correct_answer":"48 hours","explanation":"The Conduct of Election Rules 1961 mandates a 48-hour silence period before polling closes — during which all election campaign activities including processions, rallies, and canvassing are prohibited under Section 126 of the RPA.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The ECI's 'C-VIGIL' app was launched for:","options":["Counting votes electronically","Citizens to report election code violations with geotagged photos","Candidate verification of voter rolls","EVM management by polling staff"],"correct_answer":"Citizens to report election code violations with geotagged photos","explanation":"C-VIGIL (Citizen Vigilance app) was launched by ECI to enable citizens to instantly report violations of the Model Code of Conduct or Cash/Liquor/Freebies distribution with geotagged photos/videos. Reports trigger action within 100 minutes.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which constitutional amendment gave ECI the power to refer the question of a member's disqualification under the anti-defection law?","options":["52nd Amendment (10th Schedule) — decision is by Speaker/Chairman, not ECI","61st Amendment","91st Amendment","42nd Amendment"],"correct_answer":"52nd Amendment (10th Schedule) — decision is by Speaker/Chairman, not ECI","explanation":"Disqualification under the Tenth Schedule (anti-defection law) is decided by the Speaker/Chairman, NOT the ECI. The 52nd Amendment (1985) created this system. ECI's role in disqualification is under Article 103 (not anti-defection).","chapter_number":48,"level":1},
    {"text":"The ECI was given autonomous status constitutionally to ensure:","options":["That the ruling party controls elections through the government","Free and fair elections insulated from executive interference","That each state gets proportional representation","That only educated candidates can run for office"],"correct_answer":"Free and fair elections insulated from executive interference","explanation":"The founding fathers deliberately placed ECI outside executive control (Article 324) to ensure that the body conducting elections is independent of the government of the day — preventing ruling parties from manipulating electoral outcomes.","chapter_number":48,"level":1},

    # CH48 L2 (30)
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI's structural independence:\n1. The CEC's salary and service conditions are determined by Parliament by law — not by the executive.\n2. The CEC cannot be removed except by Parliamentary address — providing security of tenure.\n3. The two Election Commissioners have the same security of tenure as the CEC.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — ECs can be removed on the recommendation of the CEC, providing less security of tenure than the CEC. Statements 1 and 2 correctly capture ECI's constitutional protections.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Model Code of Conduct (MCC):\n1. MCC comes into force on the date elections are announced by the ECI.\n2. MCC applies to the ruling party, opposition parties, and independent candidates equally.\n3. Violation of MCC can result in criminal prosecution of the violator.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — MCC violations are not directly criminal (no statutory backing). ECI can censure, report to President/Governor, or recommend action. Direct criminal prosecution for MCC violation is not provided. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 2013 SC judgment in PUCL v. Union of India on voters' right to information:\n1. The SC held that voters have a right to know the criminal antecedents of candidates.\n2. Candidates must disclose assets, liabilities, educational qualifications, and criminal antecedents in affidavits.\n3. Failure to disclose in affidavit leads to automatic disqualification of the candidate.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — non-disclosure itself does not automatically disqualify; the candidate can be held liable for corrupt practice of undue influence. Statements 1 and 2 are correct about the PUCL/ADR judgments on candidate disclosure.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about free and fair elections as a basic structure element:\n1. The SC has held that free and fair elections are part of the basic structure of the Constitution.\n2. Parliament can constitutionally amend electoral laws to favour the ruling party.\n3. The ECI's plenary powers under Article 324 are themselves part of the basic structure.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — Parliament cannot amend electoral laws to damage free and fair elections; that would violate the basic structure. Statements 1 and 3 are correct — free and fair elections AND ECI's independent role have been held as basic structure elements.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about EVMs and constitutional challenges:\n1. EVMs have been repeatedly challenged in courts as prone to manipulation.\n2. The SC has consistently upheld EVMs as reliable and constitutionally valid.\n3. ECI has introduced VVPAT to address transparency concerns about EVMs.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. EVMs have faced repeated court challenges; SC has upheld them (including in 2024). VVPAT was introduced to add a paper audit trail to address concerns.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about Political Party registration with ECI:\n1. Registration as a political party with ECI is mandatory before contesting elections.\n2. A registered (but unrecognised) party does not get a reserved symbol nationwide.\n3. Registration can be cancelled by ECI if a party fails to contest elections over a period.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Registration is mandatory (Section 29A, RPA 1951). Unrecognised parties get a free (not reserved) symbol per constituency. ECI can deregister parties that fail to participate in elections for an extended period.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Chief Election Commissioner (Amendment) Act, 2023:\n1. The 2023 Act removed the CJI from the appointment committee for CEC and ECs.\n2. The appointment committee now comprises the PM, a Union Cabinet Minister, and the Leader of Opposition.\n3. The SC struck down the 2023 Act in its entirety.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the SC did not strike down the 2023 Act as of 2024 (the matter is sub judice). Statements 1 and 2 correctly describe the 2023 Act's key provisions.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about election disputes and Article 329:\n1. Courts cannot interfere with the electoral process once elections begin — only ECI can.\n2. An election dispute can be challenged only after results are declared, through an election petition.\n3. Article 329 bars courts from staying elections mid-process.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three correctly capture Article 329 — courts cannot interfere in elections once initiated (no injunctions to stop elections). Disputes can only be raised post-election through election petitions. Article 329 is an absolute bar on mid-election court interference.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the disqualification of elected members:\n1. The President (for MPs) and Governor (for MLAs) decide disqualification questions on the advice of ECI.\n2. ECI's opinion in disqualification cases is binding on the President and Governor.\n3. This applies to disqualification on all grounds — including the 10th Schedule (anti-defection).\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the 10th Schedule (anti-defection) disqualification is decided by the Speaker/Chairman, not ECI. ECI's opinion under Article 103/192 is binding only for other grounds. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about election expenditure monitoring by ECI:\n1. ECI deploys expenditure observers during elections to monitor candidate spending.\n2. Exceeding the prescribed expenditure limit is a corrupt practice under the RPA 1951.\n3. Parties (not just candidates) have individual expenditure limits per constituency.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — expenditure limits are per candidate, not per party per constituency. Parties have overall collection limits but not constituency-specific limits. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about simultaneous elections and ECI:\n1. ECI has expressed support for simultaneous elections to reduce election fatigue and expenditure.\n2. Simultaneous elections require constitutional amendments to synchronise terms of Lok Sabha and State Assemblies.\n3. ECI has the power to unilaterally implement simultaneous elections without Parliamentary approval.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — simultaneous elections require constitutional amendments; ECI cannot implement them unilaterally. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the concept of 'electoral roll' in India:\n1. A person is entitled to be enrolled in the electoral roll of the constituency where they ordinarily reside.\n2. A person cannot be enrolled in more than one constituency.\n3. Overseas Indians cannot be enrolled in electoral rolls under the current law.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — Overseas Electors (NRIs who have not acquired foreign citizenship) can be enrolled in the constituency of their last Indian address under the RPA 1950 (amended 2010). Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'star campaigner' concept in Indian elections:\n1. A national or state party can designate up to 40 star campaigners whose travel expenses are not counted against a candidate's expenditure limit.\n2. Star campaigners must be on the party's list submitted to ECI.\n3. Unrecognised parties cannot have star campaigners.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — unrecognised parties can also have up to 20 star campaigners (though recognised parties can have up to 40). Statements 1 and 2 are correct about the star campaigner system under ECI's rules.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the independence of India's Election Commission compared to other countries:\n1. India's ECI is often cited as one of the most independent electoral body models globally.\n2. Unlike the US Federal Election Commission, India's ECI has executive powers to conduct elections.\n3. India's ECI is under the supervision of the Ministry of Law and Justice.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — ECI is constitutionally independent; while the Law Ministry's Election Division coordinates with ECI on some matters, ECI is not 'under' any ministry. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about SC judgment in Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachilhu (1992) and elections:\n1. This case validated the 10th Schedule (anti-defection law) but struck down the provision that barred judicial review.\n2. The SC held that the Speaker's decision under the 10th Schedule is subject to judicial review.\n3. This ruling expanded ECI's jurisdiction over anti-defection cases.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — Kihoto Hollohan expanded judicial review of Speakers' decisions under the 10th Schedule; it did not expand ECI's role. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI and religious/caste appeals in elections:\n1. Section 123 of the RPA defines appeal on grounds of religion, race, caste, community as 'corrupt practice.'\n2. A candidate whose agent makes a religious appeal is liable even if candidate was unaware.\n3. ECI can disqualify a candidate for corrupt practices directly without going to court.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — ECI cannot directly disqualify for corrupt practices; only the HC (on an election petition) can disqualify and ECI/SC can consider separately. Statements 1 and 2 are correct about RPA Section 123.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about Delimitation and ECI:\n1. Delimitation boundaries once fixed by the Delimitation Commission cannot be challenged in court.\n2. ECI implements but does not create the delimitation; the Delimitation Commission is a separate statutory body.\n3. Article 329(a) bars courts from questioning the validity of delimitation of constituencies.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Article 329(a) explicitly bars courts from questioning delimited boundaries. The Delimitation Commission is separate from ECI. All three correctly describe the legal framework.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI transparency and RTI:\n1. ECI is subject to the Right to Information (RTI) Act.\n2. ECI proactively discloses election schedules, candidate affidavits, expenditure statements on its website.\n3. ECI has claimed that EVM source code is a protected trade secret exempt from RTI.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. ECI is subject to RTI. It has a robust proactive disclosure system. ECI has invoked exemptions when RTI requests for EVM technical details (source code) are made — citing manufacturer IP protection.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI's 'Systematic Voters' Education and Electoral Participation' (SVEEP) programme:\n1. SVEEP is ECI's flagship voter awareness and education programme.\n2. SVEEP focuses specifically on first-time voters aged 18-19.\n3. SVEEP includes campaigns targeting women, urban apathetic voters, and PWD voters.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — SVEEP targets all voter segments including women, youth, urban voters, PWD, elderly, and minorities — not just first-time voters. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about election petitions in India:\n1. An election petition must be presented within 45 days of the date of election of the returned candidate.\n2. Only a voter of the constituency can file an election petition.\n3. The HC trying an election petition must conclude the trial within 6 months of presentation.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — any candidate who contested that election OR an elector (voter) of the constituency can file an election petition. Statements 1 and 3 are correct under the RPA 1951.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI and the 'paid news' phenomenon:\n1. Paid news involves publishing news in favour of candidates without disclosure that it is a paid advertisement.\n2. ECI treats paid news as a violation of Section 127A of the RPA (pre-certification of election matter).\n3. ECI has successfully prosecuted several candidates for paid news in criminal courts.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — successful criminal prosecution for paid news has been limited. ECI treats it as expenditure violation (adding to candidate's account). Statements 1 and 2 correctly describe the paid news framework.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the constitutional provisions on elections:\n1. Article 324 covers ECI's powers; Article 325 prohibits exclusion of any citizen from electoral roll on grounds of religion, race, caste, or sex.\n2. Article 326 establishes adult suffrage; Article 327 enables Parliament to make laws on elections.\n3. Article 328 enables State Legislatures to make laws on elections to State Legislatures.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three correctly map the constitutional articles on elections: 324 (ECI), 325 (no exclusion), 326 (adult suffrage), 327 (Parliament's legislative power on elections), 328 (State legislatures' power for state elections).","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about Returning Officers (ROs) and Presiding Officers (POs) in elections:\n1. Returning Officers are appointed by ECI from state government officers.\n2. Presiding Officers manage individual polling stations on polling day.\n3. Neither ROs nor POs can be from the home district of the constituency to prevent local influence.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is not a universal constitutional requirement — ECI has administrative guidelines on avoiding local bias, but it is not an absolute rule that applies to all ROs/POs. Statements 1 and 2 correctly describe the roles.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about absentee/postal voting in India:\n1. Postal ballots are available to service voters (armed forces, government employees posted outside home state, etc.).\n2. Senior citizens above 80 years and PWDs can vote through postal ballots under the current framework.\n3. Overseas Indian voters can vote through postal ballot.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Postal ballots are available to service voters (RPA Section 20), to senior citizens/PWDs (ECI notified in 2019-2020), and to overseas electors (RPA Section 60).","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI and Central Armed Police Forces (CAPF) deployment:\n1. ECI can demand deployment of CAPF for election duty from the Central Government.\n2. State police forces are under direct command of ECI during election duty.\n3. ECI can transfer state police officials who are perceived to be partisan.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is partially inaccurate — state police are under state command but ECI superintends their election duties. ECI can and does request transfers of partisan officials (Statement 3 correct). Statement 1 is correct. Statement 2 overstates direct command.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'Schedule' of elections announced by ECI:\n1. Once ECI announces the election schedule, Model Code of Conduct kicks in.\n2. The election schedule cannot be revised once announced under any circumstances.\n3. ECI may extend/revise the schedule in extraordinary circumstances like natural disasters.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — ECI can and does revise election schedules in exceptional circumstances (e.g., death of a candidate, natural disaster, law and order situations). Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI's power to sanction requisition of government premises for elections:\n1. ECI can requisition school buildings, community halls, and other government premises for polling stations.\n2. Private premises cannot be requisitioned for election purposes.\n3. Compensation for use of requisitioned private premises is paid to the owner.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — private premises CAN be requisitioned for election purposes (polling booths, etc.), and compensation is paid (Statement 3 correct). Statement 1 is correct. Statements 1 and 3 together are right.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about election laws and the right to privacy:\n1. Voter data (electoral rolls) can be accessed by political parties and candidates — voters cannot opt out.\n2. ECI has guidelines on data privacy for electoral roll information.\n3. The SC's Puttaswamy (right to privacy) judgment has not affected electoral roll disclosure rules.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is inaccurate — after Puttaswamy (2017), ECI revised its data sharing guidelines and the question of electoral roll privacy is an evolving issue. Statements 1 and 2 are correct about the current framework.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about 'exit polls' and ECI regulations:\n1. Exit polls cannot be broadcast/published until the last phase of polling concludes in a multi-phase election.\n2. Pre-election opinion polls are regulated by ECI under the MCC.\n3. Conducting exit polls during active voting hours is prohibited under the RPA.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — pre-election opinion polls are not directly regulated by ECI (there is no law banning them); only MCC has advisory provisions. Statements 1 and 3 are correct — exit poll publication ban (Section 126A, RPA) applies until last polling ends.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},
]
